From 3a556a21a955b940d7e14ff3254e9b25fd8594c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Seedance: generate from ModelArk Asset Library ids, including
 verified real people
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Seedance 2.x screens every inline reference image for real people, and a
photorealistic character sheet is rejected as a possible real person even
when it is entirely AI-generated:

  InputImageSensitiveContentDetected.PrivacyInformation
  The input image content[1] may contain a real person.

No prompt wording avoids it — the check runs on the picture. The fix is
ByteDance's asset library: an image is reviewed once at upload, and
generations then carry asset://<id>, which the model already trusts.

There are two libraries with OPPOSITE requirements, and the value of this
patch is largely in keeping them apart:

  - Virtual portraits (AIGC): AI-generated characters, which must not
    resemble any real person. One group per character.
  - Real humans (LivenessFace): one verified person per group. The person
    completes ByteDance's own liveness check, which is what secures portrait
    rights at the source — a consent checkbox in our UI cannot stand in for
    it. Every upload into the group is face-matched against the reference
    captured during that check.

  - lib/ark/signer.ts   Volcengine V4 request signing for the Assets API,
                        which is a signed top-level OpenAPI service (AK/SK),
                        not the bearer token the models use.
  - lib/ark/assets.ts   Group and asset CRUD, activation polling, credential
                        storage, and the two verification actions.
  - lib/arkIdentity.ts  The verification round trip: a row exists from the
                        moment a session starts, and the tenant binding
                        travels in an HMAC-signed state because the person
                        usually finishes on a phone with no app session.
  - videoGen/providers/ark.ts
                        Direct ModelArk Seedance provider. Asset ids go out
                        as asset://<id> and are named positionally in the
                        prompt; inline images are the fallback, never the
                        default.
  - characterAssets.ts  Routes each image to the right library, or to
                        neither: an uploaded photo with no verified person
                        behind it is never sent to the virtual library,
                        because it may be a real human and those are exactly
                        what that library forbids. Characters predating
                        reference-source tracking wait for an admin.

Character clips take the asset route when the provider can use it, which also
drops the per-scene keyframe generation. Identity-locked jobs deliberately do
not fail over to providers that ignore asset ids: a substitute would return a
well-formed clip of the wrong person, which is worse than a retryable failure.

Also: admin card for credentials, verified people and per-character status
with a retry, and a backfill script for characters created before this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
