+# Seedance Asset Library — integration guide
+
+Two things in one patch, both solving the same wall:
+
+1. **Virtual portraits** — fixes `InputImageSensitiveContentDetected.PrivacyInformation` ("the input image `content[1]` may contain a real person") for AI-generated character sheets.
+2. **Real people** — lets a genuine face appear in a generated video, through ByteDance's liveness verification.
+
+## The problem
+
+Seedance 2.x screens every reference image in a generation request for real people. A photorealistic character sheet trips that filter **even when it is entirely AI-generated** — which is why Replicate and OpenRouter reject them too. No prompt wording gets around it, because the check runs on the picture.
+
+The fix is ByteDance's asset library: an image is reviewed once at upload, and generations then carry `asset://<id>`, which the model already trusts. One review per character instead of a moderation gamble per scene.
+
+There are two libraries, with **opposite** requirements, and this patch keeps them strictly apart:
+
+| | Virtual portrait library | Real-human library |
+|---|---|---|
+| Group type | `AIGC` | `LivenessFace` |
+| Holds | AI-generated characters that must **not** resemble any real person | One verified real person per group |
+| Group created by | `CreateAssetGroup` | The liveness verification flow |
+| Consent | n/a | The person passes ByteDance's own check |
+
+Asset ids are scoped to the ModelArk account that minted them. An asset created under a reseller's account (Atlas Cloud, Higgsfield) is unusable with your key, and vice versa — this only works with a direct BytePlus/ModelArk account.
+
+## Apply it
+
+```bash
+git apply --check seedance-asset-library.patch   # dry run — should print nothing
+git am seedance-asset-library.patch              # applies as one commit
+pnpm --filter @workspace/db run push             # 1 new table, 7 nullable columns; additive
+pnpm run build
+```
+
+No new npm dependencies. Generated API clients are included.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Two different credentials, and mixing them up is the most likely first failure:
+
+| What | Used for | Where |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ARK_API_KEY` (bearer token) | Generating video | Admin → video gen settings, or the env secret |
+| `ARK_ACCESS_KEY_ID` + `ARK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` (AK/SK pair) | The Assets API and verification | Admin → ModelArk Asset Library card, or the env secrets |
+
+The Assets API is a signed top-level OpenAPI service, so it needs the key pair — a bearer token will not authenticate it. For the real-person flow the key pair's account also needs **`ArkFullAccess`** on the project.
+
+Optional overrides: `ARK_BASE_URL` (default `https://ark.ap-southeast.bytepluses.com/api/v3`), `ARK_ASSETS_HOST` (default `open.byteplusapi.com`), `ARK_ASSETS_REGION` (default `ap-southeast-1`).
+
+### Prerequisites on the ModelArk account
+
+Neither can be done from code; both surface as a readable error on the admin card:
+
+1. **Advanced Creation Rights** activated. The capacity quota is shared between both libraries.
+2. **The first virtual asset group requires an authorization letter** signed in the ModelArk console.
+3. **The real-human library is invite-only.** Request access through BytePlus's enterprise channel. Until then the verification endpoints will fail upstream; nothing else is affected.
+
+### Selecting the provider
+
+Admin → video gen settings → provider `ByteDance ModelArk`. Defaults to Seedance 2.5 for both engines. Asset ids are only used on this provider; every other provider ignores them and falls back to the keyframe path, so switching back changes nothing.
+
+## Putting a real face in a video
+
+The person verifies themselves — that is what secures portrait rights at the source, and it is why a consent checkbox in KOKAO cannot stand in for it.
+
+```
+POST /api/characters/identities   { "label": "Abhilash" }
+  → { id, status: "pending", verificationUrl }
+```
+
+They open `verificationUrl` (easiest on a phone — it uses the camera), confirm the authorization statement, and complete the liveness check. ByteDance redirects their browser to:
+
+```
+/api/characters/identities/callback/<state>?bytedToken=…&resultCode=10000
+```
+
+which resolves the asset group ByteDance created for them and lands them back at `/studio?identity=verified`. From then on:
+
+```
+POST /api/characters   { "name": "Me", "sourceImagePath": "/objects/…", "identityId": 1 }
+```
+
+Both halves are required. A verified identity with a *generated* image would file an invented face under a real person's group; an upload with *no* identity has no portrait-rights basis at all. Every upload into a verified group is face-matched by ByteDance against the reference captured during the check, so one person's group can never quietly accumulate someone else's face. Images with more than one face are rejected.
+
+One group per person, forever: later looks and outfits go into the same group with no re-verification.
+
+**Notes on the flow.** The signed state travels as a **path segment**, not a query parameter — ByteDance appends its result with a literal `?`, so a callback URL that already had a query string would arrive malformed. The callback is authenticated by that HMAC-signed state (the person's phone will not carry an app session), exactly like the OAuth callbacks. `BytedToken` lives 30 minutes; the H5 link is single-use, so a failed check needs a fresh one. Verification is currently free.
+
+**What is not built:** a tenant-facing screen. The endpoints are there and the studio reads `?identity=…` on return, but wiring a button to `POST /characters/identities` and showing `verificationUrl` as a QR code is still to do.
+
+## How registration behaves
+
+Registration runs automatically and in the background when a character or costume is created. Every outcome is written to the outfit row, so failures are visible rather than silent. Which library an image may enter is the single most consequential branch:
+
+- **Linked to a verified person** → their liveness-verified group.
+- **AI-generated sheet** → a virtual portrait group, one per character.
+- **Uploaded photo, no verified person** → **nowhere.** It may well be a real human, and the virtual library forbids those. Submitting anyway would risk your account for an upload ModelArk would most likely reject.
+- **Origin unknown** (created before this patch) → not registered automatically; an admin can force it once they have confirmed the sheet is AI-generated.
+
+Generation then takes one of two routes per clip:
+
+- *Asset route* — ModelArk selected and the outfit's asset is `Active`: ids go straight into the request and **no per-scene keyframe is generated**.
+- *Keyframe route* — everything else: unchanged from today.
+
+**Failover is blocked for identity-locked jobs.** If ModelArk is down, a substitute provider would happily return a well-formed clip of the wrong person. A failed job the tenant can retry is the better outcome.
+
+**Deletes release the asset upstream** — capacity is a purchased, shared quota.
+
+## Backfill
+
+```bash
+pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server run backfill:ark-assets -- --dry-run
+pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server run backfill:ark-assets
+pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server run backfill:ark-assets -- --tenant 42
+pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server run backfill:ark-assets -- --include-legacy
+```
+
+Safe to re-run: outfits already holding an `Active` asset are skipped. Characters created before reference-source tracking are **not** registered by default — we cannot tell whether their sheet is AI-generated or a photo of a real person. `--include-legacy` overrides that once you have satisfied yourself they are all AI-generated.
+
+## Admin surface
+
+Admin → AI → **ModelArk Asset Library**: the AK/SK pair; every verified person with their status and group; and every character with its group, per-outfit asset status, and the verbatim upstream error. Characters are badged `Real person #n`, `Uploaded photo, no verified person`, or `Origin unknown` so it is obvious which will never register and why. **Register** re-runs registration for one character.
+
+## Image guidance
+
+ByteDance's own best practice, which also fixes face/costume consistency: 2+ stills per person in one group, vertical, one full-body frontal and one face close-up above the shoulders, neutral expression, face about two-thirds of the frame. Limits: jpeg/png/webp/bmp/tiff/gif/heic, aspect ratio 0.4–2.5, 300–6000 px, under 30 MB.
+
+## New API surface
+
+Tenant: `GET|POST /characters/identities`, `GET /characters/identities/callback/{state}` (public, HMAC-authenticated). `POST /characters` gains an optional `identityId`.
+
+Admin (superadmin only): `GET /admin/ark-assets`, `PUT|DELETE /admin/ark-assets/key`, `POST /admin/ark-assets/characters/{id}/register`.
+
+## Verified
+
+- Typechecks clean across libs, api-server and socialforge; spec lint clean; orval codegen additive only
+- `db push` applied cleanly against a fresh Postgres 16
+- New tests cover the signer, the provider, registration routing between the two libraries, the verification round trip (including tampered and cross-tenant callbacks), and the admin card
+
+## One thing to confirm on your first real call
+
+The prompt references reference material positionally, and BytePlus documents two spellings: the video-generation API reference uses `@Image1` in its own request body, while the real-human guide says to write `image 1`. This patch uses `@Image1`, isolated in `assetReferenceLabel()` in `videoGen/providers/ark.ts` — one line to switch if a live call disagrees.
+
+## Known limits
+
+- No tenant-facing verification screen (see above).
+- Registration status is only visible in the admin surface, not the tenant-facing characters API.
+- `waitForAssetActive` gives up after 5 minutes and records `Processing`; the admin Register button re-checks. There is no automatic re-poll sweep.
+- `DeleteAssetGroup` for a real person has upstream authorization-status restrictions; this patch only deletes individual assets.
